00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Welcome to the fourth week of this class. By now, you've seen forward propagation and

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.080
back propagation in the context of a neural network with a single hidden layer as well

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.680
as logistic regression. And you've learned about vectorization and when it's important

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.920
to initialize the weights randomly. If you've done the past couple of weeks' homeworks,

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.360
you've also implemented and seen some of these ideas work for yourself. So by now,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.840
you've actually seen most of the ideas you need to implement a deep neural network.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:30.360
What we're going to do in this week is take those ideas and put them together so

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.080
that you'll be able to implement your own deep neural network. Because this week's

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.200
programming exercise is longer and just has a bit more work, we're going to keep the

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.680
videos for this week shorter as you can get through the videos a little bit more

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.200
quickly and then have more time to do a significant programming exercise at the

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.480
end, which I hope will leave you having built a deep neural network that you feel

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:56.920
proud of. So what is a deep neural network? You've seen this picture for

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:02.640
logistic regression and you've also seen neural networks with a single hidden

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.160
layer. So here is an example of a neural network with two hidden layers and a

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.680
neural network with five hidden layers. We say that logistic regression is a very

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:21.840
shallow model, whereas this model here is a much deeper model. And shallow versus

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.560
depth is a matter of degree. So neural network with a single hidden layer, this

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.960
would be a two layer neural network. Remember, when we count layers in neural

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.600
network, we don't count the input layer, we just count the hidden layers as well

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:42.800
as the output layer. So this would be a two layer neural network is still quite

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.040
shallow, but not as shallow as logistic regression. Technically logistic

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.720
regression is a, you know, one layer neural network. But over the last several

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.440
years, the AI and the machine learning community has realized that there are

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:01.440
functions that very deep neural networks can learn that shallower models

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.320
are often unable to. Although for any given problem, it might be hard to

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.360
predict in advance exactly how deep a neural network you would want. So it

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.520
would be reasonable to try logistic regression, try one and then two hidden

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:18.760
layers and view the number of hidden layers as another hyper parameter that

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:24.960
you could try a variety of values of and evaluate on hold out cross validation

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.720
data or on your development set. Say more about that later as well. Let's now

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:34.640
go through the notation we'll use to describe deep neural networks. Here is a

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:44.360
one, two, three, four layer neural network with three hidden layers and the number of

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:50.080
units in these hidden layers are I guess five, five, three and then there's one output unit.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.240
So the notation we're going to use is going to use capital L to denote the

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.920
number of layers in the network. So in this case, L is equal to four and so

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:08.520
that's the number of layers and we're going to use n superscript L to denote

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:17.740
the number of nodes or the number of units in layer lowercase L. So if we

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:25.720
index this, the input as layer zero, this is layer one, this is layer two, this is

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:34.240
layer three and this is layer four, then we have that for example, n1, that would

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.240
be this, the first hidden layer would be equal to five because we have five hidden

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:44.320
units there. For this one we have that n2, the number of units in the second

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:54.280
hidden layer is also equal to five, n3 is equal to three and n4 which is n

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:01.760
capital L, this number of output units is equal to one because here capital L is

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:09.000
equal to four and we're also going to have here that for the input layer, n0 is

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:14.720
just equal to nx is equal to three. So that's the notation we'll use to

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:20.440
describe the number of nodes we have in different layers. For each layer L, we're also

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:32.000
going to use AL to denote the activations in layer L. So we'll see later that in

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:41.160
forward propagation you end up computing AL as the activation G applied to ZL and

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:47.800
perhaps the activation is indexed by the layer L as well. And then we'll use WL

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:58.840
to denote the weights for computing the value ZL in layer L and similarly BL is

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:04.360
used to compute ZL. Finally, just to wrap up on the notation, the input features

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:10.520
are called X but X is also the activations of layer zero. So A0 is equal

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:17.720
to X and the activation of the final layer, A capital L is equal to Y hat. So A

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:23.640
super square bracket capital L is equal to the predicted output to prediction Y

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:27.560
hat to the neural network. So you now know what a deep neural network looks

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.560
like as well as the notation we'll use to describe and to compute with deep

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.440
networks. I know we've introduced a lot of notation in this video but if you

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.480
ever forget what some symbol means, we've also posted on the course website

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:43.600
a notation sheet or notation guide that you can use to look up what these

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.880
different symbols means. Next, I'd like to describe what forward propagation in

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.840
this type of network looks like. Let's go on to the next video.
